From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: add SM8350 support

SM8350 has the Titan 580 camera subsystem: six CSIPHYs, three full IFEs
with their CSIDs, and two IFE-lite with theirs. The downstream driver
binds csid580 to the csid480 register table and builds vfe580 out of the
vfe480 top, bus and RDI tables, so the RDI path is handled by the gen2
CSID and VFE 480 code that SM8250 already uses. As on SM8250, each CSID
sits inside the register space of its VFE, 0x1200 into a full IFE and
0x200 into a lite one.

The CSIPHY is revision 1.2.3, not the 1.2.1 of SM8250, and none of the
existing tables matches it. Add lane_regs_sm8350 with the downstream 1.2.3
two-phase lane settings. The table also carries the two common register
writes downstream does that the generic sequence does not: COMMON_CTRL7
is 0x5a rather than 0x02, and COMMON_CTRL9 is 0x72.

The CSID-in-VFE address calculation treated every CSID with id 2 or
higher as lite. With three full IFEs that puts CSID2 at the lite offset,
so use the is_lite flag from the resource table instead. On SM8250 this
gives the same result, since ids 2 and 3 are its lite ones.

Clock rates come from the stock devicetree of the Galaxy Z Flip3: the
IFE clock steps are 338, 475, 600 and 720 MHz, everything else matches
SM8250. The CSIPHY regulator loads are taken from there too, 96.4 mA on
the 0.88 V rail and 54 mA on the 1.2 V rail. The interconnect paths and
bandwidths are the same as on SM8250.

The binding follows the SM8250 one with the third IFE added and
power-domain-names as on SC7280. It has no camera AHB clock, because
gcc-sm8350 keeps GCC_CAMERA_AHB_CLK enabled itself and does not export
it.

Downstream also programs CAMNOC QoS settings for Titan 580 through its
CPAS driver. CAMSS does not do that on SM8250 either, and it is not done
here.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
